# Line-ending policy. Copy this to `.gitattributes` at the project root.
#
# Why this file exists at all: the host shell scripts (`dev-entrypoint.sh`,
# `scripts/*.sh`) are executed INSIDE the Linux container through a bind mount,
# so bash parses the *working-tree* bytes, not what git stored. With
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git for Windows default) those bytes are CRLF and
# bash dies before it runs a line:
#
#     /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#     $'\r': command not found
#     set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# Pinning LF here prevents the problem for a fresh clone. It cannot repair a
# working tree that was already materialised before this file existed — git
# compares normalized content, so such a tree stays CRLF while `git status`
# reports clean. `dev-entrypoint.sh` detects that case itself and prints the
# remedy (see README.md -> Troubleshooting).

# Normalize every text file to LF in the working tree on every platform.
# Do NOT relax this to `eol=crlf` for scripts: the Linux container is what runs
# them, and a CRLF shebang line is unparseable there.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets are never normalized — a line-ending rewrite corrupts them.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.webm binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary

# Windows entry points may keep CRLF without affecting anything: PowerShell and
# cmd.exe read them, not the Linux kernel.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
